<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Lab 2, Due: October 9, 2026</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            <term>Instructions:</term> Complete the following exercises in your
            groups.
            For the write up:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        You should type up your report (a google docs version is
                        available on Blackboard).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Include screenshots of your work in Sage.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        You only need to hand in one report for your group.
                        Only include the names of students who contributed.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        This lab is graded out of 30 points.
                        Each problem will be graded out of 5 points - you earn 5
                        points by including your reasoning, following all
                        directions, and making only minor errors.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            There is a page of Sage cells available
            <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/0Ng" visual="http://gvsu.edu/s/0Ng">here</url>.
        </p>

        <p>
            We're going to use linear algebra to model a bike sharing program,
            like the one in Grand Rapids.
            To keep things simple, we're going to imagine there are only two
            locations, <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m>, where the bicycles are located.
        </p>

        <p>
            We will assume that the bicycles are rented in the morning and
            returned in the evening according to the following rules:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Of the bikes that are rented from location <m>P</m>,
                        <m>70\%</m> of them are returned to <m>P</m> and the
                        other <m>30\%</m> are returned to <m>Q</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Of the bikes that are rented from location <m>Q</m>,
                        <m>40\%</m> of them are returned to <m>Q</m> and the
                        other <m>60\%</m> are returned to <m>P</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that some morning there are 100 bicycles at <m>P</m>
                    and none at <m>Q</m>.
                    How many bicycles are at <m>P</m> the next morning and how
                    many are at <m>Q</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that some morning there are 100 bicycles at <m>Q</m>
                    and none at <m>P</m>.
                    How many bicycles are at <m>P</m> the next morning and how
                    many are at <m>Q</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now suppose that there are 100 bicycles at <m>P</m> and 200
                    at <m>Q</m> some morning.
                    How many are at each location the next morning?
                    Show your work and explain your thinking.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Now we're going to introduce some linear algebra to help us
                understand what's going on more generally.
                Suppose that <m>P_k</m> is the number of bicycles at <m>P</m> on
                the morning of day <m>k</m> and <m>Q_k</m> is the number of
                bicycles at <m>Q</m> on the morning of day <m>k</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The next morning, the number of bicycles, <m>P_{k+1}</m>, at
                    location <m>P</m> will be <m>70\%</m> of <m>P_k</m> and
                    <m>60\%</m> of <m>Q_k</m>.
                    Express this as an algebraic equation for <m>P_{k+1}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Also find an algebraic equation for <m>Q_{k+1}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now we'll bundle this together using vectors and matrices.
                    We will use the vector <m>\xvec_k = \twovec{P_k}{Q_k}</m> to
                    represent the distribution of bicycles on day <m>k</m>.
                    Using the equations for <m>P_{k+1}</m> and <m>Q_{k+1}</m>
                    that you just wrote, find a matrix <m>A</m> such that
                    <md>
                        \xvec_{k+1} = A\xvec_k.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Check your work by evaluating <m>A\twovec{100}{200}</m> and
                    comparing the result to what you found above in Problem
                    1(c).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that there are, on Monday morning, <m>800</m>
                    bicycles at <m>P</m> and <m>800</m> at <m>Q</m>.
                    Find the distribution of bicycles on Tuesday morning,
                    Wednesday morning, and Thursday morning.
                    Explain how you found these distributions.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose you find on a Saturday morning that there are
                    <m>998</m> bicycles at <m>P</m> and <m>502</m> at <m>Q</m>.
                    How many were at each location a day earlier on Friday
                    morning?
                    On Thursday morning?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Here are some vectors that are special for this matrix:
                <md>
                    \vvec_1 = \twovec{2}{1} \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \vvec_2 = \twovec{1}{-1}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate <m>A\vvec_1</m>.
                    What do you notice?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate <m>A\vvec_2</m> and compare it to <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                    What do you notice?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that the initial distribution of bicycles some
                    morning is written as a linear combination of <m>\vvec_1</m>
                    and <m>\vvec_2</m>:
                    <md>
                        \xvec_0 = c_1\vvec_1 + c_2\vvec_2.
                    </md>
                    Use the linearity principle of matrix multiplication, along
                    with that you just found out above, to explain why
                    <md>
                        \xvec_1 = c_1\vvec_1 + 0.1c_2\vvec_2.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Apply the same reasoning to write <m>\xvec_2</m> as a linear
                    combination of <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that the initial distribution of bicycles has
                    <m>800</m> at <m>P</m> and <m>700</m> at <m>Q</m>.
                    Write the vector <m>\xvec_0</m> as a linear combination of
                    <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use what you've found above to write <m>\xvec_1</m>, the
                    distribution of bicycles the next day, as a linear
                    combination of <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In the same way, write <m>\xvec_7</m>, the distribution of
                    bicycles one week later, as a linear combination of
                    <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens as time goes by?
                    That is, after a very long time, what happens to
                    <m>\xvec_k</m> when written as a linear combination of
                    <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What does this say about the ultimate distribution of
                    bicycles at locations <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Repeat this analysis from Problem 5 assuming that all
                <m>1500</m> bicycles start at <m>P</m>.
                What happens after a very long time?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>